# What the CODE graph (graphify-out/, rebuilt by the SessionStart hook) must not index.
# Same syntax as .gitignore; graphify reads both, this one last.
#
# The code graph feeds two readers: `graphify query/affected/path` (and its MCP
# server), and the code side of scm_agent/knowledge.py (`--bridge`, `implements()`).
# Both rank by label overlap, so a document that talks ABOUT a concept competes
# with the function that implements it -- and wins when it repeats the words more.
# Measured 2026-09-23 on 20 domain terms: only 48% of the code-side top-5 were
# implementation; the #1 hit for "safety stock" was a blog brief, and all three
# for "sales and operations planning" were communities of the BOOKS graph's report.
#
# Rule for adding a line: it is not Kern's code, nor a doc about Kern's code.
# Tests stay IN on purpose: they are the answer to `graphify affected X`
# ("what breaks if I change X"), and knowledge.implements() already filters them.

# Generated reports of a graph. knowledge/scm-books/GRAPH_REPORT.md indexed here
# made "Communities (206 total...)" a top-12 hub of the CODE graph.
**/GRAPH_REPORT.md

# Vendored third-party bundles (marked.min.js alone was 95 nodes).
*.min.js

# Implementation plans already executed: step lists with stale code snippets whose
# result now lives in src/. The design rationale is in docs/superpowers/specs/, kept.
docs/superpowers/plans/

# Public marketing blog (es/en duplicates of the same post + writer briefs).
documentation/blog/

# Third-party agent scaffolding. The constitution (.specify/memory/) is Kern's and
# stays; speckit's templates, scripts and workflow manifests are not.
.specify/templates/
.specify/scripts/
.specify/workflows/
.specify/integrations/
.specify/*.json
.claude/skills/speckit-*/

# Cursor-only skills restating Vandeput's theory. Theory belongs to the books graph
# (knowledge/scm-books/), where it is cited; here it outranked src/multi_echelon.py.
.cursor/
